# rule.prefer.rules-that-refout

a rule carries its cue and refs out its argument. the cue boots; the case for the cue does not.

```
rule.$directive.$topic.md               # the CUE — say, resident
define.$concept.md                      # the ARGUMENT + demos — ref
rule.$directive.$topic.example=$id.md   # ONE occurrence — ref
```

## .the test

does this fire before the prose exists (cue → say), or after a reader disputes it (argument → ref)? cannot say → argument.

| the rule — say | the define — ref |
|---|---|
| claim, one line | etymology, rejected peers |
| the test | worked pairs at length, dated measures |
| cue table, shapes table | counter-argument, argued boundary |
| `blocker:` / `nitpick:` / `false positive:` | — |

the enforcement block stays with the rule, always.

| when… | then… |
|---|---|
| you write "the reason this holds is…" | that sentence opens the define |
| a rule runs past its siblings' median | measure argument share; the excess is the case |
| a define no rule cites | orphan |
| a define sits at say / a rule sits at ref | inversion; cue must be resident |
| you would cut a cue to hit a size target | forbidden (`rule.require.a-cue-is-not-a-claim`). move the argument |

exception: a define whose subject is a form every passage takes stays resident (a form is produced, an etymology understood).

nitpick: argument paragraph in a resident brief · define at say with no argued exception · rule at ref · define no rule cites · enforcement block moved to a define.
blocker: a cue cut to reach a size target.
false positive: a form define at say, argued · a one-line ground beside a claim.

⇒ `rule.require.rules-are-clusters` · `rule.require.catalog-is-an-index`
